  "response": "Adjudicating between discrimination and occupational sorting is a problem with a long methodological pedigree. The Oaxaca\u2013Blinder decomposition was introduced in 1973; half a century later, Claudia Goldin was awarded the 2023 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences for work on women's labour market outcomes.\n\nOccupational and industry segregation accounts for a substantial share of the explained component in United States wage decompositions, as the unequal distribution of men and women across occupations in the labour market shows. At least one of two mechanisms is at work in generating the aggregate differential, and perhaps both: the unequal allocation of the sexes across occupations, or pay setting that rewards women less than comparable men at one and the same employer. Segregation operates between jobs and can be read off the composition of employment; by contrast, employers pay women less than men holding the same job within the same establishment, which lodges part of the differential inside the firm itself.\n\nThe experimental literature enters at precisely this point. Audit and correspondence experiments send employers matched pairs of fictitious job applications that are equivalent except for the applicant's signalled sex. Audit experiments are generally characterised as having weak internal validity and strong external validity. Because correspondence experiments take employer callback or interview-invitation rates as their primary outcome measure, audit designs identify discrimination at the hiring margin rather than in within-firm wage setting. Administrative payroll records, by contrast, observe only workers who were hired, producing a selection problem for inferences about discrimination. Confinement to realised hires cannot be squared with any account on which firm-level pay-setting differentials exhaust the gender pay gap.\n\nMatched employer\u2013employee administrative data allow researchers to estimate firm fixed effects in wage regressions. In such data, firm fixed effects account for the entire gender pay gap. The working resolution is therefore sequential rather than adversarial: experimental estimates discipline what happens at the point of entry, decompositions of payroll data bound the within-establishment component, and neither body of evidence serves as the arbiter of the other.\n\nThe historical record narrows the field further. Either the unexplained component of the United States gender pay gap has narrowed since 1980, or it has remained unchanged or widened since 1980 \u2014 one of these two holds of the historical series, and not both \u2014 and which of them obtains governs how much weight an account of the gap can place on sorting rather than on unequal treatment of equally productive workers.",
